-- title:
--   Adelic lift of a weight-one primitive form
-- statement:
--   Let $N\ge 1$ be a natural number, let $\psi$ be a Dirichlet character modulo $N$ with values in $\mathbb{C}$, and let $f$ be a cusp form of weight $1$ for $\Gamma_1(N)$ which is primitive with character $\psi$, i.e. $f$ is an eigenform with character $\psi$ and for every divisor $M'\mid N$ with $M'\neq N$ the eigenpacket formed by the $q$-coefficients of $f$ together with $n\mapsto\psi(n)$ does not occur at level $M'$. The assertion is that there exist a Hecke eigensystem $\Phi$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ with complex values (a nonzero level ideal of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ together with functions $v\mapsto\Phi.a\,v$, $v\mapsto\Phi.b\,v$ on the height-one spectrum) and a smooth cusp realization $R$ at the pins `productionPinsGeneral ℚ` for the central rescaling `Φ.toRawCentral` of $\Phi$, in which $\Phi.b\,v$ is replaced by $(\operatorname{absNorm} v)^{-1}\Phi.b\,v$ while the level and the $a$-table are unchanged — so $R$ carries a function on $\mathrm{GL}_2$ of the adeles of $\mathbb{Q}$ that is somewhere nonzero, a central character on the pinned central subgroup, the smooth cusp automorphy condition at those pins, right invariance under the pinned level subgroup at $\Phi.\mathrm{level}$, and, outside a finite exceptional set of finite places, the Hecke coset eigenfunction equation with eigenvalue $\Phi.a\,v$ and the central relation with eigenvalue $(\operatorname{absNorm} v)^{-1}\Phi.b\,v$ — such that four further things hold: first, the function of $R$ is exactly the weight-one adelic lift $\mathrm{weightOneLift}$ of $f$ at level the ideal generated by $N$, which on an adelic matrix admitting a weight-one decomposition at that level evaluates the weight-one slash of $f$ by the associated rational archimedean component at $i$, multiplied by its determinant, and is $0$ otherwise; second, this function is continuous (the genuineness condition at the pins); third, it is bounded on Siegel windows, meaning that for all reals $c,u,d_1,d_2$ with $c>0$ and $d_1>0$ and every finite set $T$ of adelic matrices there is a bound $C$ for its norm on the union of the right translates by elements of $T$ of the centre-cut Siegel set with parameters $c,u,d_1,d_2$; and fourth, there is a finite set $S$ of natural numbers such that for every prime $p\notin S$ with $p\nmid N$ and every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ containing $p$ one has $\Phi.a\,v =$ the $p$-th coefficient of the weight-one $q$-expansion of $f$ and $\Phi.b\,v = \psi(p \bmod N)$.
--
--   This is the adelisation step for weight one: a primitive form of weight $1$, level $N$ and nebentypus $\psi$ is realised as a continuous, Siegel-window-bounded automorphic function on $\mathrm{GL}_2$ of the adeles of $\mathbb{Q}$ whose Hecke and central eigenvalue tables reproduce the eigenpacket $(a_p(f),\psi(p))$ at almost all $p$. It is the form in which the lift is passed on to the statement producing a genuine cusp realization with newvector conductor and adelic span factorisation.
